The next lemma shows that \(a_{2}^{z_{h}}(\cdot;\,\cdot,\,\cdot)\) is "almost Lipschitz-continuous" with respect to \(z_{h}\).

**Lemma 5.3**.: _We keep the notations and assumptions of Lemma 5.2 but we suppose that both \(v_{h}\) and \(w_{h}\) belong to \(V_{h}\). For all pairs \(z_{h}=(\mathbf{z}_{h},\zeta_{h})\) and \(z_{h}^{\star}=(\mathbf{z}_{h}^{\star},\zeta_{h}^{\star})\) in \(X_{h}\), the difference \(a_{2}^{z_{h}}-a_{2}^{z_{h}^{\star}}\) satisfies:_

\[|a_{2}^{z_{h}}(u_{h};v_{h},\mathbf{w}_{h})-a_{2}^{z_{h}^{\star}}(u _{h};v_{h},\mathbf{w}_{h})|\] (5.31) \[\leq Ch^{1/2}|v_{h}|\,|\,|w_{h}|\begin{cases}\|\mathbf{u}_{h}\|_{ 0,\,4,\,\Omega}&\text{if }z_{h}^{\star}\neq u_{h}\\ \|\,\mathbf{z}_{h}-\mathbf{z}_{h}^{\star}\|_{0,\,4,\,\Omega}&\text{if }z_{h}^{ \star}=u_{h},\\ &\quad\forall u_{h},\,z_{h},\,z_{h}^{\star}\in X_{h},\quad\quad\forall v_{h}, \,w_{h}\,\in V_{h}.\end{cases}\]

